<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    A PPPC (Privacy Preferences Policy Control) profile granting the Kintsugi agent the two
    permissions remote control needs, so a support session works without anyone visiting the Mac.

    ============================================================================================
    READ THIS FIRST: the prerequisite this profile was blocked on is met, but nobody has yet
    confirmed the profile itself works on an enrolled Mac. Treat the CodeRequirement below as
    fillable and the result as unverified.
    ============================================================================================

    Every PPPC entry needs a CodeRequirement, and macOS checks it against the binary's signature
    before honouring the grant. The agent used to be signed *ad hoc*, so its designated requirement
    was a bare `cdhash` per slice — a hash of that exact build:

        # designated => cdhash H"fbb593c395e2cce91c9305cbae4316c29d309883" or cdhash H"105d13..."

    A profile written against that would have worked until the next agent release and then silently
    stopped, because `self_update` replaces the binary unattended and the new build hashes
    differently. Every released build is now signed with one long-lived certificate instead — minted
    once by packaging/create-signing-identity.sh and held only as this repository's Actions secret,
    since the release job is the only thing that builds what a host installs — so the requirement
    names the certificate and every future build satisfies it:

        $ codesign -d -r- /usr/local/bin/kintsugi-agent
        # designated => identifier "kintsugi-agent" and certificate leaf = H"<one hash, forever>"

    Read it off a host running a *released* build. A binary somebody built locally is ad-hoc signed
    (the key is not on any laptop), so its requirement is a hash and a profile written from it would
    grant nothing to anyone else.

    Paste that `designated =>` clause (without the `designated => ` prefix) into both
    CodeRequirement strings below. It is stable across releases, which is the whole property this
    profile needed.

    What is *not* established is that a PPPC profile honours a requirement naming a self-signed
    leaf. Apple's own guidance assumes a Developer ID, and this repository's certificate is not one:
    it is a self-signed code-signing certificate whose only job is to be the same certificate every
    time. Nothing about it is trusted anywhere except on the machine that signs with it — which is
    fine for TCC, since the requirement above only compares the leaf's hash, but it has not been
    tried through MDM here. Verify on one enrolled Mac before deploying fleet-wide, and read the
    note below about `kTCCServiceScreenCapture` specifically: the agent falls back to prompting the
    logged-in user, so a profile that quietly does not apply looks like an unexplained prompt rather
    than an error.

    Until it is verified, remote control still needs a human at each Mac to grant both permissions
    by hand — but only once, rather than once per release, which is what changed.

    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Two more things worth knowing before deploying this.

    **The path is the per-user LaunchAgent's, and that is the right one.** Remote control lives
    entirely in the `--agent` process (see clients/macos-agent/src/remote_control.rs): the screen
    and keyboard belong to a logged-in GUI session, which the root LaunchDaemon does not have. Both
    jobs run the same binary at the same path, so one entry covers it.

    **Screen Recording via PPPC has a history.** `kTCCServiceAccessibility` has been grantable by
    MDM for a long time and is dependable. `kTCCServiceScreenCapture` was, for several macOS
    releases, only ever settable to *denied* by a profile — Apple permits granting it from macOS 11
    onwards, and only for a device enrolled in user-approved MDM. Verify it actually takes effect on
    the macOS versions in your fleet rather than assuming: the agent checks
    `CGPreflightScreenCaptureAccess` and falls back to prompting the logged-in user, so a profile
    that silently does not apply looks like an unexplained prompt rather than an error.

    Deliver this with your MDM. Copy it to kintsugi-remote-control.mobileconfig, fill in the
    Developer ID requirement, and give it your own PayloadOrganization and fresh PayloadUUIDs
    (`uuidgen`) — a profile reusing another's UUID replaces it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>PayloadType</key>
    <string>Configuration</string>
    <key>PayloadVersion</key>
    <integer>1</integer>
    <key>PayloadIdentifier</key>
    <string>au.com.sharpblue.kintsugiagent.remote-control</string>
    <key>PayloadUUID</key>
    <string>REPLACE-WITH-A-FRESH-UUID-0001</string>
    <key>PayloadDisplayName</key>
    <string>Kintsugi Remote Control</string>
    <key>PayloadDescription</key>
    <string>Allows the Kintsugi agent to share this Mac's screen and accept remote keyboard and mouse input, once its user has consented to a session.</string>
    <key>PayloadOrganization</key>
    <string>REPLACE WITH YOUR ORGANISATION</string>

    <!-- Not removable by the user: the point of the profile is that a support session does not
         depend on someone having left it in place. This does not weaken the consent model — every
         session is still refused unless the person at the keyboard allows it (see
         dialogs::confirm_remote_control), and they can end one at any time from the menu bar. What
         this grants is the *ability* to capture and inject, not the permission to do it. -->
    <key>PayloadRemovalDisallowed</key>
    <true/>

    <key>PayloadContent</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>PayloadType</key>
            <string>com.apple.TCC.configuration-profile-policy</string>
            <key>PayloadVersion</key>
            <integer>1</integer>
            <key>PayloadIdentifier</key>
            <string>au.com.sharpblue.kintsugiagent.remote-control.tcc</string>
            <key>PayloadUUID</key>
            <string>REPLACE-WITH-A-FRESH-UUID-0002</string>
            <key>PayloadDisplayName</key>
            <string>Privacy Preferences Policy Control</string>
            <key>PayloadOrganization</key>
            <string>REPLACE WITH YOUR ORGANISATION</string>

            <key>Services</key>
            <dict>
                <!-- Keyboard and mouse injection: CGEventPost. Without this, every event this
                     agent posts is dropped silently — no error and no return code — so a session
                     shows the screen perfectly and simply ignores the mouse. See
                     clients/macos-agent/src/input_injection.rs. -->
                <key>Accessibility</key>
                <array>
                    <dict>
                        <key>Identifier</key>
                        <string>/usr/local/bin/kintsugi-agent</string>
                        <key>IdentifierType</key>
                        <string>path</string>
                        <key>CodeRequirement</key>
                        <string>REPLACE WITH THE designated =&gt; CLAUSE FROM: codesign -d -r- /usr/local/bin/kintsugi-agent</string>
                        <key>Allowed</key>
                        <true/>
                    </dict>
                </array>

                <!-- Screen capture: ScreenCaptureKit. Without this the stream produces either
                     nothing or a desktop picture with every window missing, which reads as a broken
                     agent rather than a missing permission. See
                     clients/macos-agent/src/screen_capture.rs, and the note above about how far back
                     MDM can actually grant this one. -->
                <key>ScreenCapture</key>
                <array>
                    <dict>
                        <key>Identifier</key>
                        <string>/usr/local/bin/kintsugi-agent</string>
                        <key>IdentifierType</key>
                        <string>path</string>
                        <key>CodeRequirement</key>
                        <string>REPLACE WITH THE designated =&gt; CLAUSE FROM: codesign -d -r- /usr/local/bin/kintsugi-agent</string>
                        <key>Allowed</key>
                        <true/>
                    </dict>
                </array>
            </dict>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
